      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives often argue from the absolute sanctity of human life, often based on the belief that only God has the right to end it, and that life is a divine good to be preserved always. For them, supporting medical aid in dying is seen as a direct challenge to divine law and a slippery slope toward devaluing human life in general.\n\nIn contrast, religious liberals often emphasize principles like compassion, autonomy, and the inherent dignity of the individual, arguing that a good end to a life in extreme suffering can align with God's will for a loving and caring community. They tend to prioritize the moral right of a competent individual to make deeply personal choices about the end of their own suffering.",
